### [03:45:00 UTC] Step 2/3 (revised) — Hypothesize and Predict (finding 4, coupled fix)
Input: The refuting failure above.
Action: Diagnosed root cause via a standalone script (per-4096-sample RMS profile across the failing test's quiet segment) -- confirmed the elevation is a single whole-buffer-FFT artifact: the loud segment's own global spectral content, retained by a threshold set against the whole buffer's peak, leaks via ifft's global sinusoidal basis into the temporally distant quiet segment. Revised hypothesis: applying the same peak-relative/DC-excluding/converging IST chain independently to short (block_size=8192, ~186ms), 50%-overlap sqrt-Hann-windowed blocks, reconstructed via constant-overlap-add (WOLA, weighted by actual accumulated window^2 rather than a flat scalar), localizes the peak reference per block and should resolve the leakage while leaving short (<=block_size) buffers -- all existing unit tests -- on the prior whole-buffer fast path.
Reasoning: This matches finding 4 in the dispatch prompt, now confirmed empirically necessary (not merely a nice-to-have) rather than optional/deferrable, because finding 1 is not safely shippable alone.
Output: n/a (prediction only) -- predicted re-running test_ist_no_static_floor_in_quiet_segment after block processing would show the RMS elevation confined to roughly one block width around the loud/quiet transition, not the entire quiet segment.

